## 2026-09-15: Phase 3, attention-score eviction and query-aware selection

### What the two new rungs say about the design

Phase 2 ended with a mechanical failure mode: a window keeps the end of the prompt, so a needle
anywhere else is gone. Both Phase 3 rungs break that dependence, and they break it differently.

- **Rung 5 (Quest-style selection)** retains {{ phase3.analysis.headline.quest.best_retention_below_full | pct }}
  of the full cache's NIAH accuracy at a {{ phase3.analysis.headline.quest.best_retention_budget | pct0 }}
  attended budget and  {{ phase3.analysis.summary.accuracy_at.quest.b25 | pct }} at 25%, against
   {{ phase3.analysis.summary.accuracy_at.window_sink.b25 | pct }} for window + sink at 25%. Paired over the
  same prompts it is {{ phase3.analysis.summary.paired_span.quest.min | pp }} to
  {{ phase3.analysis.summary.paired_span.quest.max | pp }} ahead of window + sink.
- **Rung 4 (H2O-style eviction)** retains {{ phase3.analysis.headline.h2o.best_retention_below_full | pct }}
  at {{ phase3.analysis.headline.h2o.best_retention_budget | pct0 }} and is
  {{ phase3.analysis.summary.paired_span.h2o.min | pp }} to {{ phase3.analysis.summary.paired_span.h2o.max | pp }}
  ahead of window + sink. Its weakness is positional: accumulated attention favours early keys, which every
  later query can see, so it keeps the start and the recent window and drops the middle
  ({{ phase3.analysis.summary.at_mid_depths.h2o.min | pct0 }}–{{ phase3.analysis.summary.at_mid_depths.h2o.max | pct0 }}
  at interior depths, against {{ phase3.analysis.summary.at_depth0.h2o.min | pct0 }}–{{ phase3.analysis.summary.at_depth0.h2o.max | pct0 }}
  at the start).

**Still nothing meets the brief's ≥ 99% retention target below a 100% budget**, and rung 5's near miss is
the interesting part: the accuracy is nearly there while the *memory* is not saved at all. Rung 5 keeps
every block resident ({{ phase3.analysis.quest.resident_fraction.min | pct0 }} of the sequence) and varies
only what it attends to, so on this rung the budget axis and the memory axis are different things. That is
exactly the gap Phase 4's CPU tier has to close: selection decides *which* blocks matter, and offload is
what turns that decision into free VRAM.

An unplanned result worth keeping: **H2O keeps the attention sink without being told to.** Block 0 stayed
resident in all {{ phase3.analysis.h2o.layers }} layers at every budget, with no sink rule in the policy.
Phase 2 had to hard-code it.

### Cost

Query-aware selection costs {{ phase3.analysis.summary.manager_ms_per_token.quest.min | .1f }}–{{ phase3.analysis.summary.manager_ms_per_token.quest.max | .1f }} ms
of host time per token and H2O {{ phase3.analysis.summary.manager_ms_per_token.h2o.min | .1f }}–{{ phase3.analysis.summary.manager_ms_per_token.h2o.max | .1f }} ms,
against {{ phase3.analysis.summary.manager_ms_per_token.window_sink.min | .1f }}–{{ phase3.analysis.summary.manager_ms_per_token.window_sink.max | .1f }} ms
for a plain window; scoring the prompt for H2O adds {{ phase3.analysis.summary.prefill_scored_over_plain | .2f }}× to prefill.
Rung 5 also gathers its attended set in every selecting layer at every step, because a sparse kernel is out
of scope, so its latency here is an upper bound on what the method costs.

### Measurement incidents (kept because they would have become findings)

1. **The full-cache reference is not a stable ruler.** Its per-repeat medians span
   {{ phase3.analysis.cross_phase.full_repeat_medians.phase3.min | ms }}–{{ phase3.analysis.cross_phase.full_repeat_medians.phase3.max | ms }}
   in Phase 3 and {{ phase3.analysis.cross_phase.full_repeat_medians.phase2.min | ms }}–{{ phase3.analysis.cross_phase.full_repeat_medians.phase2.max | ms }}
   in Phase 2, while every policy condition shared by the two phases reproduces much more tightly. Ratios
   against the full cache inherit that spread; absolute per-token times are what compares across phases.
   Cause not yet identified.
2. **Cross-phase check added rather than assumed.** Phase 3 re-measured every Phase 2 condition it compares
   against, in the same run. Quality reproduced exactly:
   {{ phase3.analysis.cross_phase.niah_scored_differently }} of
   {{ phase3.analysis.cross_phase.niah_prompt_conditions_compared }} prompt × condition pairs scored
   differently, and teacher-forced KL differed by {{ phase3.analysis.cross_phase.tf_max_abs_kl_diff | .1e }} nats.

